description:
## What
Create a RuntimeEnvironment enum in stoa_core with Production/Development/Test variants and a from_env() constructor that reads STOA_ENV. Production is the fail-safe default.

## Algorithm
1. Create crates/core/src/env.rs.
2. Define:
   pub enum RuntimeEnvironment { Production, Development, Test }
   impl RuntimeEnvironment {
     pub fn from_env() -> Self {
       match std::env::var('STOA_ENV').as_deref() {
         Ok('development') | Ok('dev') => Self::Development,
         Ok('test') => Self::Test,
         _ => Self::Production,  // fail-safe default
       }
     }
     pub fn is_dev(&self) -> bool { matches!(self, Self::Development | Self::Test) }
     pub fn as_str(&self) -> &'static str { ... }
   }
   impl Display for RuntimeEnvironment { ... }
3. Add pub mod env; and pub use env::RuntimeEnvironment; to crates/core/src/lib.rs.
4. cargo fmt --all; cargo clippy clean; cargo test clean.

## Defensive programming rules
- NEVER unwrap() the env var; use .as_deref() + match.
- The match _ arm MUST be Production (not Development) — production is the fail-safe default.
- No key material touched; no logging in this module.

## Tests (in crates/core/src/env.rs)
- test_default_is_production: with STOA_ENV unset → Production. Oracle: fail-safe default spec in epic.
- test_development_variants: STOA_ENV=development → Development; STOA_ENV=dev → Development. Oracle: epic description.
- test_test_variant: STOA_ENV=test → Test. Oracle: epic description.
- test_unknown_is_production: STOA_ENV=staging → Production (unknown = fail-safe). Oracle: fail-safe default.
- test_is_dev: Development.is_dev() == true; Production.is_dev() == false; Test.is_dev() == true. Oracle: definition.
Use std::env::set_var / remove_var in tests (serialize with #[serial_test::serial] if that dep exists, else use individual test binaries or accept the env var isolation strategy).
